// Practice quiz: Reading: final and toString
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/classes-and-objects/final-and-tostring

::o1 final method::What does final on a method stop? {
=A subclass overriding it#Correct. A final method keeps the same body in every subclass.
~Anyone calling it#Not quite. A final method can be called like any other.
~The method changing any field#Not quite. final on a method says nothing about the fields it changes.
~The method being inherited#Not quite. Subclasses still inherit it; they just cannot replace it.
}

::o1 final class::What does final on a class, as in public final class String, stop? {
~Creating objects of it#Not quite. You can create as many String objects as you like.
~Its fields from ever changing#Not quite. That depends on the fields, not on final on the class.
~Calling its methods#Not quite. Its methods can be called normally.
=Any other class extending it#Correct. No subclass of a final class can be declared.
}

::o2 final array::final int[] nums \= \{1, 2\}; Which next line fails to compile? {
=nums \= new int[3];#Correct. final stops nums from being pointed at a different array.
~nums[0] \= 9;#Not quite. The array object can still change; only the reference is fixed.
~System.out.println(nums.length);#Not quite. Reading through a final reference is always allowed.
~int first \= nums[0];#Not quite. Reading an element is allowed.
}

::o2 final list::A final variable names refers to an ArrayList of Strings. What does final guarantee? {
~The list can never change#Not quite. names.add("a") is allowed; final does not freeze the object.
=names always refers to this same list#Correct. The list can still gain and lose elements; the variable cannot be reassigned.
~Every String in the list is final#Not quite. final on the variable says nothing about the elements.
~The list is copied each time it is used#Not quite. final does not copy anything.
}

::o3 override shape::Which declaration in a class overrides toString from Object? {
~public void toString()#Not quite. toString must return a String.
=public String toString()#Correct. It matches the inherited method, so println and String joining use it.
~String toString()#Not quite. Without public it narrows the inherited access, which the compiler rejects.
~public String toString(String format)#Not quite. A parameter makes a separate overload; println still calls the inherited version.
}

::o3 why Override::Why write @Override above your toString? {
~Without it the method is never called#Not quite. The annotation does not change which method runs.
~It makes the method final#Not quite. @Override and final are unrelated.
=The compiler then reports an error if the method does not actually override one#Correct. A typo such as tostring would otherwise compile as a new, unused method.
~It is required for every method in a class#Not quite. It is optional and belongs only on methods that override.
}
